course: dictionary
lesson: 516
title: One word, many meanings
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: One word can have more than one meaning. The clues in the sentence tell you which meaning the writer chose. «Associate» is a good example. It can be a verb, a noun or an adjective, so always check the words around it.
explain: When you associate one thing with another, you connect them in your mind. I associate summer with ice cream, so when I smell ice cream, I think of summer. To associate with people means to spend time with them, and the word «associate» can also be a noun.
explain: «"Associate" often means an ordinary, low-paid employee, especially in US shops.» Here the word is a noun, a person who works somewhere. Read the whole sentence, then ask which meaning fits best.
fact: Context means the words and sentences around a word.
fact: «Associate» can be a verb, a noun or an adjective.
fact: As a verb, «associate» can mean to connect things in your mind, as in «I associate summer with ice cream».
fact: As a verb, «associate with» people means to spend time with them.
fact: As a noun, an «associate» is a person you work with or know through work.
fact: As an adjective, «associate» describes a lower or partly joined rank, as in «an associate member».
exercise: «I associate summer with ice cream.» What does associate mean here? | To fall asleep | To shout at someone | *To connect in your mind | To buy something in a shop
exercise: «Be careful who you associate with online.» What does associate with mean here? | *To spend time with | To ignore | To count up | To run away from
exercise: «Mia's associate at work helped her finish the report.» What is an associate in this sentence? | *A person you work with | A type of computer | A heavy tool | A lost dog
test: «The associate member can vote, but not yet as a full member.» What does associate describe here? | A cold day | *A lower or partly joined rank | A happy mood
test: In maths, the associative law says (2 + 3) + 4 = 2 + (3 + 4). What can you do with the numbers? | *Group adding or multiplying either way | Always subtract first | Only add the biggest number first | Change the answer each time
exam: Which sentence uses associate to mean spend time with people? | I associate summer with ice cream | An associate member can vote | Smells can make us think of old memories | *Be careful who you associate with online
